{
 "comment": "The one overworld of the game, as in Final Fantasy VI and VII (D-1242, D-1274). PR-110 lays out region one, 160 tiles wide and 128 high (D-1297). The `overworld` command of Tools writes the terrain rows, the zone grid, and the tile of each thing from `content/worldgen/overworld.json`, and a test proves that this file matches, so change the settings and never these rows by hand (D-1294, D-1295). The road climbs north from the foothills past the village to the mining town, and on to the border fort and the ice crossing. The deep mine lies west of the town, and the gorge lies east of it, with the refuge on the far cliff, which no walkable tile reaches (D-1276, D-1279, D-1280). Rock ridges, rivers, and lakes split the middle of the land, side routes lead off the road, and the road crosses a river on a bridge (D-1300 to D-1302). Each place is a mark with no link until the PR of the place makes it an entrance on the same tile, and the lead walks over a mark (D-1270 to D-1272). Four side landmarks stand off the road: a broken waystone, a dead mine head, war graves, and a bandit lookout (D-1299). Five gates hold the story: the road to the town opens when Bergit joins, the mine mouth when Ottild joins, the sealed door at the breakout, and the road up at the night pass, and the way down to the village shuts at the breakout (D-1281, D-1282). Six zones by height and cover hold a rate of 0 and no group until PR-17 writes the enemies of region one (D-1283, D-1284). Each later region adds its land at an edge of this map (D-1274).",
